+.SS "Why This Color Model?"
+A programmer new to
+.I curses
+may wonder why the library works with pairs of indexed colors
+instead of \*(``direct\*('' foreground and background RGB triples.
+The answer lies in the limited bandwidth between terminals
+and their time-sharing host machines.
+In the 1980s,
+a 9600bps serial link was considered fast.
+That speed typically corresponded to a data rate
+of 960 bytes per second.
+.\" Consider BSD curses's single "standout" attribute occupying the most
+.\" significant bit of a 7-bit ASCII-encoded character cell, and 8N1
+.\" transmission.  (There is always 1 start bit, thus 8N1 -> 10 bits.)
+Using a single-byte character encoding,
+refreshing an 80\(mu24 terminal screen took two full seconds.
+(Alternatively,
+a channel with this data payload rendered half a frame per second.)
+Adding data to each character cell
+necessary for the \*(``direct\*('' color model
+at 8 bits per color channel
+would add 6 bytes to every character cell,
+extending the full-screen refresh time to 14 seconds.
+Even a pair of 8-bit color values would triple the refresh time.
+.PP
+Moreover,
+the vast majority of
+.I curses
+applications do not demand a wide gamut of colors.
+Even a colorful application like the game \fB\%nethack\fP(6)
+renders most of its interface in monochrome by default.
+A text user interface
+employing the \fBform\fP(3X) or \fBmenu\fP(3X) libraries
+often uses fewer than ten distinct colors at any one time.
+Thus,
+small integers suffice both to index individual colors
+and to pair them.
+Typically,
+each color pair is assigned to a type of user interface element,
+like a button or scroll bar control.
+Further,
+in the original SVr3.2
+.I curses
+implementation of color and today still in
+.IR ncurses 's
+non-wide library,
+.I chtype
+affords few bits for encoding of the color pair identifier.
+.PP
+Even in the common modern scenario where a terminal emulator
+runs on the same host as the
+.I curses
+application,
+and available bandwidth is limited only by the speed of the system bus,
+efficient encoding of character cell data aids performance
+by minimizing copies to and from the kernel's memory space
+and the CPU mode switches thus demanded
+by use of the pseudoterminal (pty) system interface.
+Parsimonious data management also reduces memory cache pressure.
