+.SS "Pitfalls of Long Terminal Type Descriptions"
+Because terminal descriptions in
+.I termcap
+are limited to 1023 bytes,
+specifying many function key definitions
+or translating
+.I term\%info
+entries to
+.I termcap
+can cause problems.
+.PP
+The man pages for 4.3BSD
+and older versions of
+.B \%tgetent
+instruct the user to
+allocate a 1024-byte buffer for the
+.I termcap
+entry.
+The entry gets null-terminated by
+the
+.I termcap
+library,
+so that makes the maximum safe length for a
+.I termcap
+entry
+1KB\-1 (1023) bytes.
+Depending on what the application and the
+.I termcap
+library being used does,
+and where in the
+.I termcap
+file the terminal type that
+.B \%tgetent
+is searching for is,
+several bad things can happen:
+.bP
+some
+.I termcap
+libraries print a warning message,
+.bP
+some exit if they find an entry that's longer than 1023 bytes,
+.bP
+some neither exit nor warn, doing nothing useful, and
+.bP
+some simply truncate the entries to 1023 bytes.
+.PP
+Some application programs allocate more than
+the recommended 1K for the
+.I termcap
+entry;
+others do not.
+.PP
+Each
+.I termcap
+entry has two important sizes associated with it:
+before
+\*(``tc\*('' expansion,
+and after \*(``tc\*('' expansion.
+\*(``tc\*('' is the capability that
+tacks on another
+.I termcap
+entry to the end of the current one,
+to add on its capabilities.
+If a
+.I termcap
+entry does not use the \*(``tc\*('' capability,
+then of course the two lengths are the same.
+.PP
+The \*(``before tc expansion\*('' length is the more important one,
+because it affects more than just users of that particular terminal.
+This is the length of the entry as it exists in
+.IR /etc/termcap ","
+minus the backslash-newline pairs,
+which
+.B \%tgetent
+strips out while reading it.
+Some
+.I termcap
+libraries strip off the final newline,
+too
+(though GNU
+.I termcap
+does not).
+Now suppose:
+.bP
+a
+.I termcap
+entry before expansion is more than 1023 bytes long,
+.bP
+and the application has allocated only a 1KB buffer,
+.bP
+and the
+.I termcap
+library
+(like the one in BSD/OS 1.1 and GNU)
+reads the whole entry into the buffer,
+no matter what its length,
+to see if it is the entry it wants,
+.bP
+and
+.B \%tgetent
+is searching for a terminal type that either is the long entry,
+appears in the
+.I termcap
+file after the long entry,
+or
+does not appear in the file at all (so that
+.B \%tgetent
+has to search the whole
+.I termcap
+file).
+.PP
+Then
+.B \%tgetent
+will overwrite memory,
+perhaps its stack,
+and probably core dump the program.
+Programs like \fI\%telnet\fP(1) are particularly vulnerable;
+modern
+.IR telnet s
+pass along values like the terminal type automatically.
+The results are almost as undesirable with a
+.I termcap
+library,
+like those of SunOS 4.1.3 and Ultrix 4.4,
+that prints warning messages when it reads an overly long
+.I termcap
+entry.
+If a
+.I termcap
+library truncates long entries,
+like OSF/1 3.0,
+it is immune to dying here
+but will return incorrect data for the terminal.
+.PP
+The \*(``after tc expansion\*('' length will have a similar effect to the
+above, but only for people who actually set
+.I TERM
+to that terminal type,
+since
+.B \%tgetent
+only does \*(``tc\*('' expansion
+once it has found the terminal type it was looking for,
+not while searching.
+.PP
+In summary,
+a
+.I termcap
+entry that is longer than 1023 bytes can cause,
+with various combinations of
+.I termcap
+libraries and applications,
+a core dump,
+warnings, or incorrect operation.
+If it is too long even before \*(``tc\*('' expansion,
+it will have this effect even for users of some other terminal types
+and users whose
+.I TERM
+variable does not have a
+.I termcap
+entry.
+.PP
+When in \-C
+(translate to
+.IR termcap )
+mode,
+the
+.I \%ncurses
+implementation of \fB@TIC@\fP(1M) issues warning messages
+when the pre-tc length of a
+.I termcap
+translation is too long.
+The \-c (check) option also validates resolved
+(after tc expansion)
+lengths.
